## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of least fixed-point computation in monotone boolean networks with thresholds and how to implement an efficient propagation algorithm that converges even with cycles.

## Key Concepts
- Monotone operators and least fixed points
- Threshold-based activation with in-neighbor counting
- Queue-driven BFS-like propagation
- Handling cycles via convergence to a fixpoint

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation of the propagation
function leastFixedPoint(T, edges, thresh) {
  const n = thresh.length;
  const succ = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  for (const [u,v] of edges) succ[u].push(v);
  const count = Array(n).fill(0);
  const inSet = Array(n).fill(false);
  const q = [];
  for (const i of T) { inSet[i] = true; q.push(i); }
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    for (const v of succ[u]) {
      count[v]++;
      if (!inSet[v] && count[v] >= thresh[v]) {
        inSet[v] = true;
        q.push(v);
      }
    }
  }
  return Array.from({length: n}, (_,i) => i).filter(i => inSet[i]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for weighted thresholds or non-monotone edges?
- How would you prove the result is the least fixed point containing T?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 3-valued semantics in robotics sensor fusion and implement a concrete propagator for arbitrary rule sets.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic (T>U>F)
- Propagation rules for ¬, ∧, ∨, and → (X→Y ≡ ¬X ∨ Y)
- Backtracking to avoid F while preserving at least one U

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple 3-valued implication using Kleene logic
function implies(x, y){
  const not = v => v === 'T' ? 'F' : v === 'F' ? 'T' : 'U';
  const or = (a,b) => (a==='T' || b==='T') ? 'T' : (a==='U' || b==='U') ? 'U' : 'F';
  return or(not(x), y);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to larger graphs with hundreds of variables?
- What heuristics help prune search effectively in 3-valued backtracking?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests reasoning with Kleene 3-valued logic on implications and disjunctions, plus a concrete backtracking strategy that preserves non-F constraints while allowing Unknowns.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued semantics
- Implication as ¬A ∨ B
- Propagation and pruning in backtracking
- Constructing a concrete satisfying assignment with U

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal 3-valued logic helpers
const order = {F:0, U:1, T:2};
function not(v){ return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U';}
function or(a,b){ return order[a] >= order[b] ? a : b; }
function and(a,b){ return order[a] <= order[b] ? a : b; }
function implies(a,b){ return or(not(a), b); }
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to detect unsatisfiable constraint sets with a fixed number of U?
- How would you implement a backtracking search that guarantees termination in finite time?
